<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1949 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Auferstanden aus Ruinen</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The national anthem of East Germany from 1949 until Germany was united again in 1990.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Johannes Becher</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Hanns Eisler</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the German Democratic Republic (East Germany)</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>German Democratic Republic (East Germany)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1949</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words by Johannes Becher, music by Hanns Eisler (1949))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auferstanden-aus-ruinen">Auferstanden aus Ruinen</a>&quot; (&quot;Risen from Ruins&quot;) was the national anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">East Germany</a>. In 1949 the Soviet occupation zone of Allied-occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> became a socialist state, the German Democratic Republic (GDR). For its anthem the poet Johannes Becher, who later became the East German Minister of Culture, wrote the words. Two musicians, Ottmar Gerster and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanns-eisler">Hanns Eisler</a>, proposed settings, and Eisler's was chosen.</p><p>Written just after the GDR was proclaimed, the anthem reflects the first years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s division and of the country rising from the ruins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, when most Germans still expected the occupation zones to be reunited. Becher's text therefore speaks repeatedly of unity and of a single German fatherland. These <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">themes</a> soon sat badly with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, above all after the East German government built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall">Berlin Wall</a> in 1961.</p><p>In September 1973 East and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> were admitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> at the same time, after talks that gave a degree of mutual recognition. The word &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>&quot; was later removed from the East German constitution, and on official occasions only the anthem's tune was played. No new words were ever written to replace Becher's, which went on being sung unofficially, especially after the Peaceful Revolution (die Wende) of late 1989.</p><p>The song ceased to be an anthem when the GDR was dissolved and its states joined the Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> at reunification in 1990, and the &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deutschlandlied">Deutschlandlied</a>&quot; became the anthem of united Germany. The East German premier Lothar de Maizière proposed adding Becher's words to the united anthem; the West German chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmut-kohl">Helmut Kohl</a> rejected the idea. In November 1995 the anthem was played by mistake during a visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> by the German president Roman Herzog, the first time it had been played at an event since reunification. In July 2026 Tino Chrupalla, co-chairman of the far-right Alternative for Germany, and a candidate, Ulrich Siegmund, sang it together at a campaign event for the 2026 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a> state election.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A national anthem of the new East German state, on the theme of a country rebuilding after the war and of a united German fatherland; solemn and hopeful.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so the Institute does not print them itself: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="443838" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/Johannes-r-becher-auferstanden-aus-ruinen-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
